WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.132
Entitlement management automates access
requests, assignments, reviews and

2
00:00:05.132 --> 00:00:08.964
expiration for groups,
applications and SharePoint sites,

3
00:00:08.964 --> 00:00:11.075
for internal and external users.

4
00:00:11.075 --> 00:00:12.962
It has three building blocks.

5
00:00:12.962 --> 00:00:16.198
A catalog is the container
and the unit of delegation;

6
00:00:16.198 --> 00:00:20.535
the program creates one per business
domain, with the domain's business

7
00:00:20.535 --> 00:00:24.725
owner as catalog owner, and doesn't
use the automatic General catalog.

8
00:00:24.725 --> 00:00:27.537
An access package bundles resource roles.

9
00:00:27.537 --> 00:00:32.501
And a policy says who may request, who
approves, and when the assignment ends.

10
00:00:32.501 --> 00:00:37.425
The approval chains follow the application's
risk tier, as design positions.

11
00:00:37.425 --> 00:00:39.116
Tier 3: the department owner,

12
00:00:39.116 --> 00:00:44.262
which is the requestor's manager with a
fallback approver for anyone with no manager

13
00:00:44.262 --> 00:00:47.790
in the directory, seven days to
decide, and a one-year expiry

14
00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:51.613
that extends without approval
while the user is still in scope.

15
00:00:51.613 --> 00:00:56.145
Tier 2: the application owner, with
the backup owner as alternate,

16
00:00:56.145 --> 00:00:58.625
five days, a hundred and eighty days.

17
00:00:58.625 --> 00:01:00.410
Tier 1 adds a second stage,

18
00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:04.575
the identity governance office,
and expires in ninety days.

19
00:01:04.575 --> 00:01:08.555
The earlier procedure only notified
the office of Tier 1 grants;

20
00:01:08.555 --> 00:01:13.587
a second stage costs a few hours and leaves
an approval record instead of an email.

21
00:01:13.587 --> 00:01:18.038
External users get a separate,
stricter policy and at most a year.

22
00:01:18.038 --> 00:01:22.838
The program never uses "never."
Underneath is the group substrate.

23
00:01:22.838 --> 00:01:26.938
Applications are assigned to
groups, never to users directly.

24
00:01:26.938 --> 00:01:28.788
Nested groups are prohibited,

25
00:01:28.788 --> 00:01:33.747
because group assignment to an application
gives access only to direct members,

26
00:01:33.747 --> 00:01:38.188
and a group review shows nested users
but can only remove direct members.

27
00:01:38.188 --> 00:01:43.263
And names follow one convention, so a
group's purpose and tier can be read from it.

28
00:01:43.263 --> 00:01:48.286
Separation of duties: a package can
list incompatible packages and groups,

29
00:01:48.286 --> 00:01:52.175
but the check is one-way, so each
package must list the other.

30
00:01:52.175 --> 00:01:55.081
A legitimate exception
becomes its own combined

31
00:01:55.081 --> 00:01:58.412
package with a shorter expiry
and more frequent reviews.

32
00:01:58.412 --> 00:02:01.785
And privileged access arrives
through PIM eligibility,

33
00:02:01.785 --> 00:02:04.850
never through a package
that grants an active role.
